<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  examples/hardened-webview/network_security_config.xml
  Compiled into the hardened lab APK as res/xml/network_security_config.xml.

  No cleartext anywhere, system anchors only, no debug overrides. This is the
  configuration a WebView-hosting app should ship, and chapter 44 explains every
  element: ../../../44-NetworkSecurityConfig/README.md.

  Read it together with HardenedWebViewActivity.java, because the pair is the
  whole point of this chapter's boundary:

    this file       decides WHOSE certificates count, and whether plaintext HTTP
                    may leave the device at all. It reaches inside the WebView:
                    Chromium consults NetworkSecurityPolicy through JNI and
                    refuses an http:// load with ERR_CLEARTEXT_NOT_PERMITTED.

    the Java file   decides what happens when validation FAILS. That decision is
                    onReceivedSslError, it lives in code, and nothing in this
                    file constrains it. An app can ship this exact config and
                    still accept every forged certificate on the internet.

  Which is why an audit that reads only one of the two is not an audit.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

</network-security-config>
